<h1>SolarScan AI — Simple Project Summary</h1>
<p><strong>BSc Information Technology Final Year Project</strong><br>
University of Energy and Natural Resources (UENR), Ghana</p>

<p><em>This simple guide explains the core parts of your project so you can easily talk about it in your presentation tomorrow.</em></p>

<hr>

<h2>1. The Problem Statement (Why We Built This)</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Solar energy is growing:</strong> Clean electricity from solar panels is crucial for homes and businesses, especially in remote regions.</li>
    <li><strong>Panels get damaged:</strong> Solar panels sit outdoors. Over time, they get dirty (dust/soiling), cracked (micro-cracks), or dangerously overheated (hotspots).</li>
    <li><strong>Loss of energy and money:</strong> A damaged panel produces much less electricity. This causes massive power drops and costs solar owners billions of dollars globally.</li>
    <li><strong>Existing solutions are too expensive:</strong> Traditional ways to inspect panels require buying $5,000 thermal cameras, hiring drones, or using high-end lab setups.</li>
    <li><strong>Internet dependency:</strong> Most AI diagnostic tools require a fast internet connection to upload photos, which is impossible in off-grid rural areas.</li>
    <li><strong>The Solution:</strong> We created <strong>SolarScan AI</strong> — a smart mobile app that scans panels and detects faults instantly <strong>on the device without needing the internet</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2. The Methodology (How We Built It)</h2>
<p>We followed 5 simple steps to design, train, and test this system:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Step 1 — Gathered Panel Images:</strong> We collected 4,312 photos of solar panels from online research libraries and actual field testing. These included healthy panels as well as panels with dust, cracks, and hotspots.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 2 — Trained the AI Brain:</strong> We used a state-of-the-art computer vision model called <strong>YOLOv8</strong>. We showed it thousands of images until it learned to automatically recognize and draw red boxes around panel defects.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 3 — Compressed the AI for Mobile Phones:</strong> A normal AI model is too big (6.3 Megabytes or more) and runs slowly on a phone. We compressed our model down to just <strong>3.2 Megabytes</strong> (using a process called quantization). Now, it runs on standard mobile processors in under 0.2 seconds without any lag.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 4 — Designed the Mobile App:</strong> We built a clean, visual interface. A technician simply opens the app, points the phone camera at a solar panel, and the app instantly draws boxes around any faults and tells them how to fix it.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 5 — Usability Testing:</strong> We had 12 real solar technicians and students test the app. They gave the system a usability score of <strong>84.3 out of 100</strong>, which means the app is highly intuitive and easy to navigate in the field.</li>
</ol>

<h2>3. Significance of the Project (Why It Matters)</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Empowers local technicians:</strong> Instead of relying on expensive tools, local installers in Ghana can diagnose solar faults using their normal mobile phones.</li>
    <li><strong>Works in remote villages:</strong> Because the AI model runs offline on the device, technicians can inspect panels in deep off-grid locations with zero mobile network connection.</li>
    <li><strong>Increases solar lifespan:</strong> Catching cracks and hotspots early allows operators to wash or repair panels before they break permanently, saving thousands of dollars in replacement costs.</li>
    <li><strong>High Accuracy:</strong> Our compressed AI model achieves **92.7% accuracy** (mAP@50), proving that we do not have to sacrifice accuracy to make the software run offline on a phone.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Easy Q&A Cheat Sheet (For answering questions from the examiners)</h2>

<div class="qa-box">
    <p><strong>Examiner Question: "How does the app work offline?"</strong></p>
    <p><em>Your Answer:</em> The AI model is stored directly inside the mobile app. When you take a photo, the phone's processor runs the calculation locally. It doesn't send the photo to a server over the internet, making it 100% offline.</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
    <p><strong>Examiner Question: "What are the common defects the app can find?"</strong></p>
    <p><em>Your Answer:</em> It identifies 8 main classes: Hotspots (overheating spots), Micro-cracks, Dust/Soiling, Delamination (layers peeling apart), Snail Trails (oxidation lines), Cell Discoloration (aging), Potential Induced Degradation (voltage leakage), and Bypass Diode circuit failures.</p>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
    <p><strong>Examiner Question: "How did you make sure the app is user-friendly?"</strong></p>
    <p><em>Your Answer:</em> We followed standard user interface principles like Norman's guidelines (clear visual feedback) and tested it with 12 real users. The app achieved a System Usability Scale (SUS) score of 84.3, which represents "Excellent" usability.</p>
</div>

